00:09:02.661 --> 00:09:04.005
Here is a quick table:

150
00:09:04.285 --> 00:09:06.055
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

151
00:09:06.335 --> 00:09:07.445
Live delivery.

152
00:09:07.725 --> 00:09:08.557
Choice:

153
00:09:08.837 --> 00:09:09.647
Hybrid:

154
00:09:09.927 --> 00:09:18.205
WebRTC or SFU for the teacher and on-stage students; LL-HLS or CMAF via CDN for the mass audience.

155
00:09:18.485 --> 00:09:19.167
Why:

156
00:09:19.447 --> 00:09:27.469
WebRTC to 100k viewers is costly and hard to scale; streaming scales cheaply with 2 to 5 s latency.

157
00:09:27.749 --> 00:09:28.773
Chat at scale.

158
00:09:29.053 --> 00:09:29.885
Choice:

159
00:09:30.165 --> 00:09:34.090
Sampled or aggregated fan-out, not every message to everyone.

160
00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:35.053
Why:

161
00:09:35.333 --> 00:09:41.327
5k msgs per second times 100k viewers is impossible and unreadable.

162
00:09:41.607 --> 00:09:42.653
Test delivery.

163
00:09:42.933 --> 00:09:43.765
Choice:

164
00:09:44.045 --> 00:09:50.146
Pre-download encrypted paper; key release at start; autosave each answer; submit is a marker.

165
00:09:50.426 --> 00:09:51.109
Why:

166
00:09:51.389 --> 00:09:54.823
Removes start and end spikes, protects against disconnects.

167
00:09:55.103 --> 00:09:55.893
Timing.

168
00:09:56.173 --> 00:09:57.005
Choice:

169
00:09:57.285 --> 00:10:02.191
Server-authoritative start and end; client shows a countdown synced to server time.

170
00:10:02.471 --> 00:10:03.154
Why:

171
00:10:03.434 --> 00:10:06.271
Prevents clock tampering (I02 L1).

172
00:10:06.551 --> 00:10:07.618
Evaluation.

173
00:10:07.898 --> 00:10:08.730
Choice:

174
00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:11.399
Asynchronous, idempotent, per candidate.

175
00:10:11.679 --> 00:10:12.362
Why:

176
00:10:12.642 --> 00:10:15.245
Handles 500k submissions calmly.

177
00:10:16.595 --> 00:10:17.917
CBT centres.

178
00:10:18.197 --> 00:10:19.029
Choice:

179
00:10:19.309 --> 00:10:24.771
Local server per centre with encrypted papers and local response storage, synced centrally.

180
00:10:25.051 --> 00:10:25.733
Why:

181
00:10:26.013 --> 00:10:28.339
Survive internet loss; security.

182
00:10:29.689 --> 00:10:30.606
Part 4.

183
00:10:30.886 --> 00:10:32.763
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

184
00:10:34.013 --> 00:10:37.661
Journey 1: a live physics class for 60,000 students.

185
00:10:37.941 --> 00:10:51.040
Before class: enrolled students get a reminder 15 minutes before;. the class page loads from the CDN;. the app fetches a join token (entitlement check: is the student in this batch or subscription?).

186
00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:59.171
Teacher goes live: the teacher's studio app sends camera, screen or tablet and audio over WebRTC to an SFU;.

187
00:11:00.521 --> 00:11:15.369
a composer mixes the teacher video and slides or whiteboard into one layout, or keeps them as separate tracks;. it encodes an ABR ladder and packages LL-HLS (CMAF chunks);. the CDN serves 60,000 students.

188
00:11:15.649 --> 00:11:23.969
Latency is ~2 to 5 s, while mobile viewers on 3G get lower renditions (I06 §6.3).

189
00:11:24.249 --> 00:11:30.393
Whiteboard: strokes are sent as vector events (not video) over WebSockets, and rendered on devices;.

190
00:11:31.743 --> 00:11:38.527
this is crisp at any resolution and needs little bandwidth;. it is synced with the video timeline via timestamps.

191
00:11:38.807 --> 00:11:49.644
Chat (§5.2): students send messages to the chat service through WebSocket gateways;. messages are moderated (profanity filters, rate limits per student);.

192
00:11:50.994 --> 00:12:07.015
the service samples what to fan out: every student sees a manageable stream (for example, 5 messages per second), with teacher and moderator messages and top questions pinned;. all messages are stored for the teacher and moderators to see and search.

193
00:12:07.295 --> 00:12:09.877
Doubts: students can mark a message as a doubt.

194
00:12:10.157 --> 00:12:16.237
Similar doubts are clustered (text similarity), and moderators surface the top clusters to the teacher.

195
00:12:16.517 --> 00:12:20.357
Polls: the teacher launches a poll (What is the SI unit of force?).

196
00:12:21.707 --> 00:12:36.192
60,000 answers arrive in 20 seconds (~3k per second);. aggregated in memory per option, and the running result is broadcast every second;. individual answers are stored for analytics and student reports.

197
00:12:36.472 --> 00:12:39.651
Hand raise: the teacher invites a student on stage.

198
00:12:39.931 --> 00:12:47.803
That student joins the SFU with WebRTC for sub-second interaction, and their video is composed into the stream for everyone else.

199
00:12:49.153 --> 00:12:58.305
After class: the recording (the composed stream, or tracks plus whiteboard events for interactive playback) is packaged as VOD within minutes.

200
00:12:58.585 --> 00:13:02.702
Attendance, watch time, poll accuracy and doubts go to analytics.

201
00:13:04.052 --> 00:13:07.487
Journey 2: an all-India mock test at 10 am Sunday.

202
00:13:07.767 --> 00:13:25.900
Before the test: the test is assembled from the question bank according to a blueprint: sections, topics, difficulty distribution, marks scheme (§6.3);. variants may be created (shuffled order, different question sets of equal difficulty) to reduce copying;. from 9:30,

203
00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:33.391
registered students' apps pre-download the encrypted test package from the CDN: questions, images, instructions.

204
00:13:33.671 --> 00:13:35.591
Downloads spread over 30 minutes.

205
00:13:35.871 --> 00:13:36.660
Start:

206
00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:44.623
at 10:00:00 (server time), the app requests the decryption key and a start token.

207
00:13:44.903 --> 00:13:56.999
The server records the start time for that candidate;. late joiners get the time remaining for their window, per test rules;. key requests are small, and served by a scaled, cached key service.

208
00:13:57.279 --> 00:14:00.373
During the test: each answer change is autosaved:

209
00:14:00.653 --> 00:14:06.498
(candidate, test, question, answer, client sequence, client time) goes to the autosave service.

210
00:14:06.778 --> 00:14:10.405
It is written to a durable store and acknowledged, showing saved;.

211
00:14:11.755 --> 00:14:23.381
if offline, answers queue locally and sync when the connection returns, with sequence numbers resolving order (§6.8);. the timer shows a countdown synced to server time.

212
00:14:23.661 --> 00:14:29.059
The server's recorded start plus duration defines the end, regardless of the client clock.

213
00:14:29.339 --> 00:14:35.440
End: at the end time the app auto-submits: a small submitted marker plus any unsynced answers.

214
00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:41.843
The server rejects answers timestamped after the end (with tolerance for network delay, per rules);.

215
00:14:43.193 --> 00:14:47.609
candidates who lost connectivity have their last synced answers evaluated.

216
00:14:47.889 --> 00:14:54.374
Their buffered answers are accepted if their sequence and time prove they were answered before the end (policy).

217
00:14:54.654 --> 00:15:11.934
Evaluation: asynchronous workers score each candidate per the marks scheme (+4 or minus 1, partial marks for multi-correct; VERIFY exam patterns), idempotently;. within minutes, results: score, section scores, time analysis, accuracy by topic.

218
00:15:13.284 --> 00:15:27.727
Ranks: all-India rank and percentile among 500k candidates come from a score distribution (§6.6);. ranks become final after a cut-off time, when late submissions and re-evaluations are done.

219
00:15:29.077 --> 00:15:32.469
Journey 3: a national CBT at an exam centre.

220
00:15:32.749 --> 00:15:50.242
Weeks before: the question papers for each shift are set by experts, reviewed and stored encrypted;. centres are prepared: networked computers, a local server, power backup, CCTV, and a biometric or photo verification set-up (VERIFY specific procedures).

221
00:15:50.522 --> 00:15:56.090
Day before or morning of: the encrypted paper for the shift is downloaded to the local server;.

222
00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:02.667
the decryption key is released only shortly before the exam starts, through a secure channel.

223
00:16:02.947 --> 00:16:06.381
Keys may be split among officials (VERIFY procedures).

224
00:16:06.661 --> 00:16:11.269
Candidate entry: verified by admit card, photo and biometrics.

225
00:16:11.549 --> 00:16:17.928
Each is assigned a computer, which boots into a locked-down exam client (no other apps, no internet).

226
00:16:18.208 --> 00:16:27.253
During the exam: each candidate's responses are saved to the local server continuously, with a local audit log (every click with a timestamp);.

227
00:16:28.603 --> 00:16:40.934
the local server replicates to a second local disk or server (VERIFY);. if a computer fails, the candidate moves to a spare, and the session resumes from the server with remaining time preserved.

228
00:16:41.214 --> 00:16:57.299
After the shift: response files are encrypted and signed, then uploaded to central over the network (or physically transported if needed);. central verifies checksums and signatures, and reconciles counts: candidates present versus response files.

229
00:16:58.649 --> 00:17:12.004
Keys and scoring: provisional answer keys and candidates' response sheets are published;. candidates can challenge keys, for a fee, within a window;. experts review; final keys are published;. scoring runs.

230
00:17:13.354 --> 00:17:22.570
Normalisation: multiple shifts had different papers, so raw scores are converted to percentile scores within each shift (§6.5).

231
00:17:22.850 --> 00:17:30.786
Merit lists are formed from the normalised scores per the methodology (VERIFY the agency's published rules, including tie-breaking).

232
00:17:31.066 --> 00:17:38.042
Results: published for a million candidates on one day, via a CDN-cached results site with candidate login.

233
00:17:38.322 --> 00:17:44.295
Expect traffic spikes of tens of thousands of requests per second (pre-generated result documents).

234
00:17:45.645 --> 00:17:48.120
Journey 4: remote-proctored online exam.

235
00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.813
Setup: the candidate installs a lockdown browser or app.

236
00:17:52.093 --> 00:17:59.112
It checks the environment: a single screen, no virtual machines, no screen sharing or remote desktop tools.

237
00:17:59.392 --> 00:18:04.789
Identity: face photo matched to the registration photo, and ID document capture.

238
00:18:05.069 --> 00:18:08.995
Room scan: a 360 degrees camera sweep of the room.

239
00:18:09.275 --> 00:18:10.405
During the exam:

240
00:18:11.755 --> 00:18:36.225
AI proctoring analyses webcam and microphone streams at the edge or server (§6.7): face absent, multiple faces, gaze away repeatedly, voices, mobile phone detected, tab switching attempts;. events are flagged with timestamps and short clips;. live proctors monitor many candidates (for example, 1:20) with AI flags prioritised, and can message or terminate.

241
00:18:36.505 --> 00:18:41.689
Review: flags are reviewed after the exam, and decisions are recorded with evidence.

242
00:18:41.969 --> 00:18:46.726
Recordings are retained for a limited period per policy and consent (VERIFY).

243
00:18:48.076 --> 00:18:50.572
Journey 5: adaptive practice and doubts.

244
00:18:50.852 --> 00:18:53.604
Adaptive practice: a student opens Practice:

245
00:18:53.884 --> 00:18:54.865
Kinematics.

246
00:18:55.145 --> 00:18:59.348
The learner model estimates mastery per concept (§6.2).

247
00:18:59.628 --> 00:19:10.529
The recommender picks questions near the student's ability, just hard enough, mixing weak concepts and spaced revision of earlier ones (Ch 59 L4 principle).

248
00:19:10.809 --> 00:19:13.156
Each answer updates mastery immediately.

249
00:19:13.436 --> 00:19:16.913
Doubt solving: the student photographs a question from a textbook.

250
00:19:17.193 --> 00:19:22.377
OCR handles printed text and math notation (math OCR to LaTeX).

251
00:19:23.727 --> 00:19:29.295
Math-aware text embeddings are used for semantic search over millions of solved questions.

252
00:19:29.575 --> 00:19:36.018
If a near-duplicate exists with high confidence, the student sees the solution video and steps instantly.

253
00:19:36.298 --> 00:19:41.077
Otherwise the doubt goes to a tutor queue (chat), with subject routing and SLA.

254
00:19:42.427 --> 00:19:43.387
Part 5.

255
00:19:43.667 --> 00:19:45.203
Core Subsystems in Depth.

256
00:19:46.453 --> 00:19:48.245
Live class delivery at scale.

257
00:19:48.525 --> 00:19:49.506
Why hybrid:

258
00:19:49.786 --> 00:19:57.253
WebRTC via SFUs gives sub-second latency but needs server resources per viewer and complex scaling;.

259
00:19:57.533 --> 00:20:09.095
LL-HLS via CDN gives 2 to 5 s latency and scales to millions at low cost;. classes need low latency only for the teacher and a few on-stage students.

260
00:20:09.375 --> 00:20:14.538
Small classes (up to a few hundred) can be all-WebRTC for full interactivity.

261
00:20:14.818 --> 00:20:16.482
Big classes use the hybrid.

262
00:20:17.832 --> 00:20:35.752
Synchronising interaction with video delay: a poll the teacher launches at time T arrives at viewers' screens with the video at T + 3 s;. send poll events with a video timestamp, so clients show the poll when their playback reaches that moment;. the same applies to whiteboard events.

263
00:20:36.032 --> 00:20:40.832
Teacher-side reliability: the teacher's upload is the single point of failure.

264
00:20:41.112 --> 00:20:49.347
Use a backup encoder path, an internet quality check before class, and fallback to audio + whiteboard if the video uplink degrades.

265
00:20:50.697 --> 00:20:52.937
Chat and interaction at 100k.

266
00:20:53.217 --> 00:20:54.027
Ingest:

267
00:20:54.307 --> 00:21:02.009
WebSocket gateways (sharded by connection), then chat service partitioned by class, then storage (append-only per class).

268
00:21:02.289 --> 00:21:22.385
Fan-out: never broadcast every message to every viewer;. the class room service selects a stream per interval: teacher and moderator messages always; a sample of student messages (rate-limited per interval); highlighted doubts;. the selected stream is broadcast via gateways, one message per interval per connection batch.

269
00:21:23.735 --> 00:21:30.113
Reactions and poll votes are aggregated as counts per interval (I06 §6.8).

270
00:21:30.393 --> 00:21:35.407
Moderation: profanity and spam filters, per-user rate limits, slow mode, bans.

271
00:21:35.687 --> 00:21:44.519
Doubt clustering: embed doubt messages, cluster them online (incremental clustering), and show the teacher a ranked list of clusters with counts.

272
00:21:45.869 --> 00:21:46.914
Question bank.

273
00:21:47.194 --> 00:21:58.394
Each question has: text (with LaTeX), images, options, correct answers;. solution (text or video);. topic tags (syllabus hierarchy);. difficulty labels;.

274
00:21:58.674 --> 00:22:02.663
IRT parameters (§6.1);. exposure counts;.

275
00:22:04.013 --> 00:22:09.112
version history;. review status (created, then reviewed, then approved, then retired).

276
00:22:09.392 --> 00:22:15.707
Search and assembly: index by topic, difficulty, type and exposure, for assembly and practice.

277
00:22:15.987 --> 00:22:27.037
Quality loop: item statistics from tests (difficulty, discrimination, distractor analysis) flag bad questions: ambiguous, wrong key, or too easy or hard.

278
00:22:28.387 --> 00:22:29.987
Test engine and autosave.

279
00:22:30.267 --> 00:22:39.462
Test definitions: sections, timers (overall and per section), navigation rules, marking schemes, question pools or fixed papers, variants.

280
00:22:39.742 --> 00:22:44.649
Delivery package: encrypted per test (and per variant), downloadable in advance.

281
00:22:44.929 --> 00:22:46.529
The key is released at start.

282
00:22:46.809 --> 00:22:50.435
Optional per-candidate watermarking of content deters leaks.

283
00:22:50.715 --> 00:22:58.523
Autosave store: writes keyed by (test, candidate, question) with the highest sequence number wins;. partitioned by candidate;.

284
00:22:59.873 --> 00:23:11.671
a durable, replicated key-value store (for example, Cassandra or ScyllaDB or DynamoDB-style) handles high write rates;. acknowledgements only after a durable write.

285
00:23:11.951 --> 00:23:14.511
Submission: a marker plus a final flush.

286
00:23:14.791 --> 00:23:17.159
Evaluation is triggered via an event.

287
00:23:17.439 --> 00:23:20.084
Evaluation: idempotent per candidate.

288
00:23:20.364 --> 00:23:27.745
Recomputable if the answer key changes (§4.3 step 6), so keep responses separately from scores.

289
00:23:29.095 --> 00:23:30.930
Results, ranks and analytics.

290
00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:40.298
Per-candidate results: score, section-wise breakdown, time spent per question, accuracy by topic, comparison to toppers and averages.

291
00:23:40.578 --> 00:23:48.301
Ranks: computed from the score distribution after the test window closes (§6.6), and published once stable.

292
00:23:48.581 --> 00:23:53.637
Teacher and parent reports: batch performance, weak topics, attendance, engagement.

293
00:23:54.987 --> 00:23:56.523
CBT centre system.

294
00:23:56.803 --> 00:24:12.995
Local server: receives the encrypted paper;. holds decryption keys only at exam time;. serves the exam client to all terminals;. stores responses with an audit trail;. replicates locally;. uploads signed response packages after the shift.

295
00:24:14.345 --> 00:24:22.238
Exam client: locked-down OS image, no internet, timer from the local server, and seamless resume on terminal failure.

296
00:24:22.518 --> 00:24:31.990
Monitoring: central dashboards show centre readiness (downloads done, clocks synced, terminals up), candidates present, and upload status after shifts.

297
00:24:32.270 --> 00:24:36.131
CCTV and observers are part of the process (VERIFY).

298
00:24:37.481 --> 00:24:38.420
Part 6.

299
00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:39.660
Algorithms.

300
00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:43.171
Item response theory (IRT).

301
00:24:43.451 --> 00:24:48.870
The model: the probability that a student with ability θ answers item i correctly.

302
00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:52.329
In the 3-parameter logistic (3PL) model:

303
00:24:52.609 --> 00:25:11.745
P(correct θ) = ci + (1 minus ci) / (1 + e^(minus ai(θ minus bi))). where: bi is difficulty;. ai is discrimination (how sharply the item separates abilities);. ci is the guessing floor (for example, ~0.25 for four options).

304
00:25:13.095 --> 00:25:23.441
Calibration: estimate item parameters from many students' responses by maximum likelihood (EM or marginal maximum likelihood) or Bayesian methods.

305
00:25:23.721 --> 00:25:28.201
Ability estimation: estimate θ for each student from their responses.

306
00:25:28.481 --> 00:25:36.695
Uses: scores comparable across different question sets; question quality checks; adaptive testing; better practice recommendations.

307
00:25:38.045 --> 00:25:39.218
Knowledge tracing.

308
00:25:39.498 --> 00:25:55.839
Bayesian Knowledge Tracing (BKT): per concept, a hidden mastered or not state, with parameters for: initial knowledge;. learning rate (probability of learning after each attempt);. slip (wrong answer despite mastery);. guess (right answer without mastery).

309
00:25:56.119 --> 00:25:59.533
After each answer, update P(mastered) by Bayes' rule.

310
00:26:00.883 --> 00:26:09.181
Deep knowledge tracing: sequence models over the student's full interaction history predict the next answer's correctness across concepts.

311
00:26:09.461 --> 00:26:18.677
Uses: mastery bars in the app; choosing the next question; deciding when to revise (spaced repetition, with intervals growing as mastery holds).

312
00:26:20.027 --> 00:26:21.819
Test assembly under a blueprint.

313
00:26:22.099 --> 00:26:44.137
Constraints: number of questions per topic and section;. difficulty distribution (for example, 30% easy, 50% medium, 20% hard, or a target test information curve with IRT);. question types;. no overlapping or duplicate concepts;. exposure limits (don't reuse recent questions);. variants with equal difficulty.

314
00:26:45.487 --> 00:26:56.196
Methods: integer programming (select binary xi for each question to satisfy constraints and hit target information);. or greedy heuristics with repair.

315
00:26:56.476 --> 00:27:01.425
Parallel forms (variants) are generated so their test characteristic curves match.

316
00:27:02.775 --> 00:27:05.463
Computerised adaptive testing (CAT).

317
00:27:05.743 --> 00:27:06.447
Loop:

318
00:27:06.727 --> 00:27:08.285
Start with a medium item.

319
00:27:08.565 --> 00:27:10.591
After each answer, update θ.

320
00:27:10.871 --> 00:27:20.087
Choose the next item with maximum information at the current θ, subject to content constraints and exposure control (randomisation among top items).

321
00:27:20.367 --> 00:27:25.039
Stop when the standard error is small enough, or after a maximum number of items.

322
00:27:26.389 --> 00:27:29.120
Benefits: shorter tests with equal precision.

323
00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:32.557
Used in some standardised exams (VERIFY which).

324
00:27:33.907 --> 00:27:36.126
Percentile normalisation across shifts.

325
00:27:36.406 --> 00:27:40.395
The problem: a multi-shift exam uses different papers per shift.

326
00:27:40.675 --> 00:27:43.939
One shift may be harder, so raw scores aren't comparable.

327
00:27:44.219 --> 00:28:07.345
Percentile method (as published by some national agencies; VERIFY details): within each shift, a candidate's percentile score = 100 times (number of candidates in that shift with raw score at most the candidate's score) / (total candidates in the shift);. computed to several decimal places;. merit is based on percentile scores, with tie-breaking rules;.

328
00:28:08.695 --> 00:28:15.927
the assumption is that ability distributions are similar across shifts, so candidates are randomly allocated to shifts.

329
00:28:16.207 --> 00:28:25.849
Alternatives: equipercentile equating, and IRT-based equating using common (anchor) items, which is more robust if shift populations differ.

330
00:28:26.129 --> 00:28:32.764
Systems view: sort each shift's scores;. compute the cumulative distribution;. map each candidate's score;.

331
00:28:34.114 --> 00:28:40.194
publish with full precision and audit;. make it reproducible from stored responses and final keys.

332
00:28:41.544 --> 00:28:43.464
Ranking half a million candidates.

333
00:28:43.744 --> 00:28:55.115
Scores are discrete (for example, minus 90 to 360), so ranks come from a count histogram: rank(score) = 1 + number of candidates with a higher score.

334
00:28:55.395 --> 00:29:00.045
This is O(number of distinct scores) and trivially fast, even for millions.

335
00:29:00.325 --> 00:29:15.280
Live leaderboard during a test window: maintain counts per score bucket in a sharded counter store (Ch 51);. a candidate's provisional rank = the sum of counts above their score;. top-100 lists from a sorted set.

336
00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:20.467
Ties: break by time taken, accuracy, or sectional scores per test rules.

337
00:29:21.817 --> 00:29:23.545
Proctoring anomaly detection.

338
00:29:23.825 --> 00:29:42.043
Signals: face detection (none, multiple);. face match drift (a different person);. head pose and gaze away (away for longer than N seconds, repeatedly);. object detection (phone, book);. audio (speech detection, multiple voices);. screen events (focus loss, attempts to open apps).

339
00:29:43.393 --> 00:29:51.479
Scoring: a weighted sum of events over time windows with decay; thresholds for a live proctor alert versus a post-exam review.

340
00:29:51.759 --> 00:29:57.540
Fairness: tune for lighting and skin tone variations, network conditions and disabilities.

341
00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:00.273
Always have human review before penalties.

342
00:30:01.623 --> 00:30:02.946
Autosave ordering.

343
00:30:03.226 --> 00:30:07.002
Per question, the client increments a sequence number for each change.

344
00:30:07.282 --> 00:30:13.191
The server stores the answer with the highest sequence (last write by sequence, not by arrival order).

345
00:30:13.471 --> 00:30:16.223
Offline queue replay sends changes in order.

346
00:30:16.503 --> 00:30:18.210
The server ignores stale ones.

347
00:30:18.490 --> 00:30:26.895
The end-time rule: changes with client sequence after the recorded end, or arriving after end + grace, are rejected (policy).

348
00:30:27.175 --> 00:30:30.546
Evidence: the audit log stores every change for disputes.

349
00:30:31.896 --> 00:30:33.752
Collusion and cheating detection.

350
00:30:34.032 --> 00:30:43.632
Answer similarity: pairs of candidates with unusually many identical wrong answers (shared wrong answers are much stronger evidence than shared right ones).

351
00:30:43.912 --> 00:30:48.520
Statistical indices compare to the expected overlap given their abilities.

352
00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:53.045
Compute within centres, labs, or IP subnets to limit pair counts.

353
00:30:53.325 --> 00:31:02.349
Timing patterns: answers entered at identical times across candidates, or extremely fast correct answers on hard items (possible leak).

354
00:31:02.629 --> 00:31:07.685
Score jumps: sudden large improvements versus history (EdTech mock tests).

355
00:31:09.035 --> 00:31:12.065
Outcome: flags, then review, then action per rules.

356
00:31:12.345 --> 00:31:16.441
Evidence only, never automatic penalties for high-stakes exams.

