Choice:

460
00:34:55.109 --> 00:34:57.093
Iceberg + Spark + Trino.

461
00:34:57.373 --> 00:34:58.183
Notes:

462
00:34:58.463 --> 00:34:59.423
Analytics.

463
00:34:59.703 --> 00:35:00.855
Psychometrics.

464
00:35:01.135 --> 00:35:01.967
Choice:

465
00:35:02.247 --> 00:35:06.471
IRT libraries (Python or R), custom calibration jobs.

466
00:35:06.751 --> 00:35:07.562
Notes:

467
00:35:07.842 --> 00:35:09.271
Question calibration.

468
00:35:09.551 --> 00:35:10.255
Apps.

469
00:35:10.535 --> 00:35:11.367
Choice:

470
00:35:11.647 --> 00:35:14.165
Native Android (low-end friendly), web.

471
00:35:14.445 --> 00:35:15.255
Notes:

472
00:35:15.535 --> 00:35:16.922
India's device mix.

473
00:35:17.202 --> 00:35:18.525
CBT centres.

474
00:35:18.805 --> 00:35:19.637
Choice:

475
00:35:19.917 --> 00:35:23.437
Hardened Linux images, local servers, signed packages.

476
00:35:23.717 --> 00:35:24.527
Notes:

477
00:35:24.807 --> 00:35:25.746
Security.

478
00:35:27.096 --> 00:35:27.992
Part 9.

479
00:35:28.272 --> 00:35:30.512
Security, Privacy and Compliance.

480
00:35:31.762 --> 00:35:44.071
Children's data: verifiable parental consent, no tracking-based advertising to children, and limits on behavioural monitoring under the DPDP Act (VERIFY rules for children's data).

481
00:35:44.351 --> 00:35:50.239
Exam integrity: encrypted papers;. just-in-time keys;. access logs;. watermarking;.

482
00:35:51.589 --> 00:35:52.869
lockdown clients;.

483
00:35:53.149 --> 00:36:06.333
CCTV at centres;. chain-of-custody for response data;. penalties for leaks under applicable public examination laws (for example, anti-cheating legislation for public exams; VERIFY).

484
00:36:06.613 --> 00:36:14.869
Proctoring: transparent disclosure and consent, minimal data, short retention, human review, and accommodations for disabilities.

485
00:36:16.219 --> 00:36:25.841
Payments and refunds: clear refund policies for courses (consumer protection; VERIFY), and EMI and loan disclosures when financing courses.

486
00:36:27.191 --> 00:36:28.044
Part 10.

487
00:36:28.324 --> 00:36:30.244
Reliability and Operations.

488
00:36:31.494 --> 00:36:42.886
Fixed-time events: mock tests and national exams are scheduled, so pre-scale and pre-download;. rehearse with load tests at full concurrency;. freeze changes on test days.

489
00:36:43.166 --> 00:36:50.697
Graceful handling of disconnects: autosave and local queues, resume with remaining time, and server-authoritative timers.

490
00:36:50.977 --> 00:36:52.171
Live classes:

491
00:36:53.521 --> 00:36:59.964
pre-class teacher connectivity checks;. backup encoder paths;. a degraded mode (audio + whiteboard);.

492
00:37:00.244 --> 00:37:02.633
CDN capacity for the day's schedule.

493
00:37:02.913 --> 00:37:09.889
Results day: pre-generate result pages; serve via CDN with login tokens; queue traffic if needed.

494
00:37:11.239 --> 00:37:21.650
CBT centres: readiness dashboards, spare terminals, power backups, and contingency plans for centre failures (rescheduling candidates; VERIFY policies).

495
00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:24.003
Part 11.

496
00:37:24.283 --> 00:37:25.328
Key Trade-offs.

497
00:37:26.578 --> 00:37:27.922
Here is a quick table:

498
00:37:28.202 --> 00:37:30.719
Decision, and Chosen, and Accepted cost.

499
00:37:30.999 --> 00:37:32.109
Live delivery.

500
00:37:32.389 --> 00:37:33.221
Chosen:

501
00:37:33.501 --> 00:37:36.871
WebRTC for stage, LL-HLS for audience.

502
00:37:37.151 --> 00:37:38.325
Accepted cost:

503
00:37:38.605 --> 00:37:40.909
2 to 5 s delay for most viewers.

504
00:37:41.189 --> 00:37:41.893
Chat.

505
00:37:42.173 --> 00:37:43.005
Chosen:

506
00:37:43.285 --> 00:37:44.479
Sampled fan-out.

507
00:37:44.759 --> 00:37:45.933
Accepted cost:

508
00:37:46.213 --> 00:37:48.581
Not every message visible to everyone.

509
00:37:48.861 --> 00:37:49.842
Test start.

510
00:37:50.122 --> 00:37:50.954
Chosen:

511
00:37:51.234 --> 00:37:52.898
Pre-download + key release.

512
00:37:53.178 --> 00:37:54.351
Accepted cost:

513
00:37:54.631 --> 00:37:57.554
Complexity; security of pre-positioned content.

514
00:37:57.834 --> 00:37:58.794
Autosave.

515
00:37:59.074 --> 00:37:59.906
Chosen:

516
00:38:00.186 --> 00:38:01.743
Per-answer durable writes.

517
00:38:02.023 --> 00:38:03.197
Accepted cost:

518
00:38:03.477 --> 00:38:04.693
Higher write load.

519
00:38:04.973 --> 00:38:06.103
Normalisation.

520
00:38:06.383 --> 00:38:07.215
Chosen:

521
00:38:07.495 --> 00:38:09.970
Percentiles within shifts (as mandated).

522
00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:11.423
Accepted cost:

523
00:38:11.703 --> 00:38:13.901
Assumes comparable shift populations.

524
00:38:14.181 --> 00:38:15.375
Adaptive learning.

525
00:38:15.655 --> 00:38:16.487
Chosen:

526
00:38:16.767 --> 00:38:18.687
IRT + knowledge tracing.

527
00:38:18.967 --> 00:38:20.141
Accepted cost:

528
00:38:20.421 --> 00:38:21.999
Calibration data needed.

529
00:38:22.279 --> 00:38:23.197
Proctoring.

530
00:38:23.477 --> 00:38:24.309
Chosen:

531
00:38:24.589 --> 00:38:26.573
AI flags + human review.

532
00:38:26.853 --> 00:38:28.026
Accepted cost:

533
00:38:28.306 --> 00:38:29.991
Cost; privacy concerns.

534
00:38:31.341 --> 00:38:32.280
Part 12.

535
00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:33.904
Interview Q and A.

536
00:38:35.154 --> 00:38:35.986
Beginner.

537
00:38:36.266 --> 00:38:37.290
Question 1.

538
00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:40.962
How would you stream a live class to 100,000 students?

539
00:38:41.242 --> 00:38:42.607
Thirty-second answer:

540
00:38:42.887 --> 00:39:03.794
The teacher sends video over WebRTC to an SFU; it's composed, encoded into an ABR ladder and packaged as low-latency HLS served by a CDN to the audience with 2 to 5 s latency; only on-stage students use WebRTC; whiteboard and polls travel as small events over WebSockets.

541
00:39:05.144 --> 00:39:06.147
Question 2.

542
00:39:06.427 --> 00:39:10.139
How do you make sure a student's answers aren't lost in an online test?

543
00:39:10.419 --> 00:39:11.784
Thirty-second answer:

544
00:39:12.064 --> 00:39:24.331
Autosave each answer change to a durable, replicated store with a sequence number; show saved only after acknowledgement; queue locally when offline and sync later; submission just marks the end.

545
00:39:25.681 --> 00:39:26.726
Question 3.

546
00:39:27.006 --> 00:39:29.737
Why should test timers be server-authoritative?

547
00:39:30.017 --> 00:39:31.382
Thirty-second answer:

548
00:39:31.662 --> 00:39:44.185
Client clocks can be wrong or manipulated; the server records each candidate's start and computes the end, while the client shows a countdown synced to server time; late answers are rejected per rules.

549
00:39:45.535 --> 00:39:46.601
Question 4.

550
00:39:46.881 --> 00:39:48.823
How is an all-India rank computed?

551
00:39:49.103 --> 00:39:50.468
Thirty-second answer:

552
00:39:50.748 --> 00:40:04.551
After the test window closes, count how many candidates scored higher using a histogram of scores (scores are discrete), apply tie-break rules, and compute percentiles; provisional ranks can update live from bucket counts.

553
00:40:05.901 --> 00:40:06.903
Intermediate.

554
00:40:07.183 --> 00:40:08.293
Question 5.

555
00:40:08.573 --> 00:40:13.181
How do you handle 500,000 students starting a test at the same second?

556
00:40:13.461 --> 00:40:14.826
Thirty-second answer:

557
00:40:15.106 --> 00:40:29.698
Pre-download the encrypted paper from the CDN over the preceding 30 minutes, then release a small decryption key and start token at start time from a scaled key service; record server-side start times; late joiners get remaining time.

558
00:40:31.048 --> 00:40:32.157
Question 6.

559
00:40:32.437 --> 00:40:35.509
How would you design chat for a class of 100k?

560
00:40:35.789 --> 00:40:37.155
Thirty-second answer:

561
00:40:37.435 --> 00:40:53.733
WebSocket gateways, then chat service partitioned by class, then append-only storage; moderation and rate limits; fan out only a sampled stream plus teacher and moderator messages and pinned doubts; aggregate reactions; cluster doubts for the teacher.

562
00:40:55.083 --> 00:40:56.171
Question 7.

563
00:40:56.451 --> 00:40:59.054
What is item response theory and why use it?

564
00:40:59.334 --> 00:41:00.699
Thirty-second answer:

565
00:41:00.979 --> 00:41:17.897
A model of the probability of a correct answer as a function of student ability and item parameters (difficulty, discrimination, guessing); it makes scores comparable across different question sets, reveals bad questions, and powers adaptive testing and practice.

566
00:41:19.247 --> 00:41:20.271
Question 8.

567
00:41:20.551 --> 00:41:22.748
How does doubt solving from a photo work?

568
00:41:23.028 --> 00:41:24.393
Thirty-second answer:

569
00:41:24.673 --> 00:41:40.375
Math-aware OCR turns the photo into text and LaTeX, an embedding model searches millions of solved questions for near-duplicates, high-confidence matches return solutions instantly, and others go to a tutor queue routed by subject.

570
00:41:41.725 --> 00:41:42.791
Question 9.

571
00:41:43.071 --> 00:41:45.781
How do you assemble a fair test from a question bank?

572
00:41:46.061 --> 00:41:47.426
Thirty-second answer:

573
00:41:47.706 --> 00:42:02.319
Define a blueprint (topics, sections, difficulty or target information curve, question types, exposure limits) and select questions via integer programming or heuristics, generating equal-difficulty variants when needed.

574
00:42:03.669 --> 00:42:04.437
Senior.

575
00:42:04.717 --> 00:42:05.763
Question 10.

576
00:42:06.043 --> 00:42:09.435
How do you normalise scores across multiple exam shifts?

577
00:42:09.715 --> 00:42:11.080
Thirty-second answer:

578
00:42:11.360 --> 00:42:32.821
Compute percentile scores within each shift, 100 times candidates with score at most yours ÷ total in shift, to high precision and rank on those, as some national agencies do (VERIFY); alternatives are equipercentile or IRT equating with anchor items; make it reproducible from stored responses and final keys.

579
00:42:34.171 --> 00:42:35.302
Question 11.

580
00:42:35.582 --> 00:42:37.545
How would you build adaptive practice?

581
00:42:37.825 --> 00:42:39.190
Thirty-second answer:

582
00:42:39.470 --> 00:42:57.283
Estimate mastery per concept with knowledge tracing (BKT or deep models) and ability with IRT; choose next questions near the learner's ability, mixing weak concepts and spaced revision; update after each answer; evaluate by learning gains, not engagement alone.

583
00:42:58.633 --> 00:42:59.721
Question 12.

584
00:43:00.001 --> 00:43:02.391
How do you detect collusion in online tests?

585
00:43:02.671 --> 00:43:04.036
Thirty-second answer:

586
00:43:04.316 --> 00:43:20.935
Compare answer patterns within centres, labs or networks, focusing on shared wrong answers relative to expected overlap given abilities; look at synchronised timing and implausibly fast correct answers on hard items; flag for human review with evidence.

587
00:43:22.285 --> 00:43:23.565
Question 13.

588
00:43:23.845 --> 00:43:25.423
Design remote proctoring.

589
00:43:25.703 --> 00:43:27.069
Thirty-second answer:

590
00:43:27.349 --> 00:43:47.253
Lockdown client with environment checks, identity verification and room scan, on-device or server AI detecting absence, multiple faces, gaze, objects and voices, weighted scoring with live proctor alerts and post-exam review, consent and short retention, fairness tuning and human decisions.

591
00:43:48.603 --> 00:43:49.797
Question 14.

592
00:43:50.077 --> 00:43:53.960
How do you keep polls and whiteboard in sync with a delayed video stream?

593
00:43:54.240 --> 00:43:55.605
Thirty-second answer:

594
00:43:55.885 --> 00:44:08.536
Timestamp interactive events with the video timeline (not wall clock) and have clients display them when playback reaches that timestamp; aggregate poll results server-side and broadcast periodically.

595
00:44:09.886 --> 00:44:10.782
Architect.

596
00:44:11.062 --> 00:44:12.257
Question 15.

597
00:44:12.537 --> 00:44:15.161
Design a national computer-based test system.

598
00:44:15.441 --> 00:44:16.806
Thirty-second answer:

599
00:44:17.086 --> 00:44:39.678
Encrypted papers pre-positioned on hardened local centre servers with just-in-time key release, locked-down terminals, continuous local response saving with audit and replication, signed upload after shifts, provisional keys and challenges, scoring, percentile normalisation across shifts, and CDN-served results, with readiness monitoring and contingencies.

600
00:44:41.028 --> 00:44:42.223
Question 16.

601
00:44:42.503 --> 00:44:45.468
How would you scale an EdTech platform for exam season?

602
00:44:45.748 --> 00:44:47.113
Thirty-second answer:

603
00:44:47.393 --> 00:45:02.903
Forecast concurrency for tests and classes, pre-scale APIs, autosave stores and realtime gateways, pre-position test packages and schedule CDN capacity, stagger non-essential jobs, and rehearse with full-concurrency load tests.

604
00:45:04.253 --> 00:45:05.597
Question 17.

605
00:45:05.877 --> 00:45:08.991
What data platform would you build for learning analytics?

606
00:45:09.271 --> 00:45:10.637
Thirty-second answer:

607
00:45:10.917 --> 00:45:29.370
Events from classes, videos, practice and tests into Kafka and a lakehouse; IRT calibration and knowledge tracing training jobs feeding the question bank and adaptive service; warehouse marts for teacher, parent and business dashboards; content quality analytics; strict handling of minors' data.

608
00:45:30.720 --> 00:45:31.893
Question 18.

609
00:45:32.173 --> 00:45:35.395
How would you evaluate whether adaptive learning actually works?

610
00:45:35.675 --> 00:45:37.040
Thirty-second answer:

611
00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:52.573
Randomised experiments comparing learning outcomes (post-test gains, exam results), not just engagement; control for prior ability; measure retention over time; watch for unintended effects like frustration or dropout.

612
00:45:53.923 --> 00:45:55.161
Question 19.

613
00:45:55.441 --> 00:45:59.174
What are the security risks of online exams, and the countermeasures?

614
00:45:59.454 --> 00:46:00.819
Thirty-second answer:

615
00:46:01.099 --> 00:46:20.449
Paper leaks (encryption, JIT keys, access logs, watermarking), impersonation (identity checks, face match), device cheating (lockdown clients, proctoring), collusion (answer similarity analytics), tampering with responses (signed packages, audit logs), and insider risk (separation of duties).

616
00:46:21.799 --> 00:46:22.908
Question 20.

617
00:46:23.188 --> 00:46:25.769
Compare live classes with live sports streaming.

618
00:46:26.049 --> 00:46:27.415
Thirty-second answer:

619
00:46:27.695 --> 00:46:46.852
Both use ABR streaming via CDNs; classes need two-way interaction (polls, doubts, on-stage students) synced to a delayed stream, smaller audiences per stream but many concurrent streams, and teacher-side uplink reliability, sports needs extreme single-event scale, ads and rights.

620
00:46:48.202 --> 00:46:49.247
Part 13.

621
00:46:49.527 --> 00:46:50.295
Summary.

622
00:46:51.545 --> 00:46:53.167
Live classes are hybrid:

623
00:46:53.447 --> 00:46:59.655
WebRTC for the teacher and on-stage students, LL-HLS via CDN for the audience.

624
00:46:59.935 --> 00:47:04.009
Chat is sampled; polls and whiteboard are events synced to video time.

625
00:47:04.289 --> 00:47:14.337
Online tests remove the start and end spikes with pre-downloaded encrypted papers, key release at start, per-answer autosave and asynchronous evaluation.

626
00:47:14.617 --> 00:47:16.473
Timers are server-authoritative.

627
00:47:16.753 --> 00:47:27.889
High-stakes CBTs rely on local centre servers, just-in-time keys, audit trails, signed uploads, answer key challenges and percentile normalisation across shifts.

628
00:47:29.239 --> 00:47:36.663
Psychometrics and ML (IRT, CAT, knowledge tracing, blueprint assembly) make tests fair and practice adaptive.

629
00:47:36.943 --> 00:47:41.061
Integrity uses collusion analytics and proctoring with human review.

630
00:47:41.341 --> 00:47:46.034
Children's privacy and exam integrity are design constraints, not add-ons.

631
